e product root. Check `git ls-files --stage -- .the-way` and `git -C .the-way rev-parse HEAD`: both must show the same commit. If initialization fails or the commits differ, stop.\n+\n+Read `.the-way/product-guidelines/testing/SKILL.md` in full and follow it, and read a file it names inside `.the-way` when the step or variant that names it comes, not all of them at the start. Where it says where the-way lies, the product\'s case applies: here the process root is `.the-way`. Do not substitute a global skill.\n+\n+Where this skill or a file it reads names another the-way skill — `record-decision`, `record-invariant`, `record-glossary-term`, `update-part-map`, `record-environment-fact`, `find-foundation-entries`, `define-project`, `plan-project`, `lead-project`, `settle-technology`, `refine-task`, `implement-task`, `plan-task`, `write-spec`, `make-change`, `review-change`, `critique`, `review-foundation`, `maintain-styleguide`, `maintain-coding-guidelines` or `maintain-testing-guidelines` — use this product\'s project skill of the same name with the `the-way-` prefix, never an unprefixed one.\nCREATE .claude/agents/the-way-stage.md\n--- /dev/null\n+++ .claude/agents/the-way-stage.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@\n+---\n+name: the-way-stage\n+description: The agent of one stage of a task cycle of the-way, such as the designer of a spec, the planner, the executor of a slice, the agent of a fix or a reviewer, which has every tool of the session except the skill tool. Dispatched only by the task cycle of the-way.\n+disallowedTools: Skill\n+---\n+\n+You are the agent of one stage of a task cycle of the-way: you design or review a spec, plan a task, make a slice of a change or fix a review\'s findings, or review a change. The orchestrator of the cycle dispatched you with a handoff file that names your stage, its boundary, what you read and the path of your report.\n+\n+You have every tool of the session except the skill tool, so the method of your stage is a file you read, not a skill you call. Read the method file your handoff names whole, follow it, and return what it tells you to return.\nCREATE .claude/agents/the-way-author.md\n--- /dev/null\n+++ .claude/agents/the-way-author.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@\n+---\n+name: the-way-author\n+description: The one author of a change in a task cycle of the-way, resumed after each review for a fix and for the return step, with a prompt cache that lives an hour and every tool of the session except the skill tool. Dispatched only by the task cycle of the-way.\n+disallowedTools: Skill\n+experimental:\n+  cacheTtl: 1h\n+---\n+\n+You are the one author of a change in a task cycle of the-way: you make the whole change of one task without a plan, write the foundation entries the task carries, and take the return step. The orchestrator of the cycle dispatched you with a handoff file that names your boundary, what you read and the path of your report, and it resumes you after each review for a fix and for the return step.\n+\n+Your prompt cache lives an hour, so that a review between your turns does not make you write your whole context to the cache again. You have every tool of the session except the skill tool, so the method of your stage is a file you read, not a skill you call. Read the method file your handoff names whole, follow it, and return what it tells you to return.\nAPPEND AGENTS.md\n(added at the end; the existing content stays byte for byte)\n+\n+<!-- the-way:begin -->\n+# Pinned the-way process, changed\n+\n+This repository uses the-way, the process for its foundation, at the commit the gitlink of the Git submodule `.the-way` records. After a fresh clone run `git submodule update --init -- .the-way`; it needs read access to the Git URL in `.gitmodules`. Before use, `git -C .the-way rev-parse HEAD` must show the commit `git ls-files --stage -- .the-way` records. If initialization fails or the commits differ, stop; never use another copy or version of the-way.\n+\n+Write foundation entries only through the project skills `the-way-record-decision`, `the-way-record-invariant`, `the-way-record-glossary-term`, `the-way-update-part-map`, `the-way-record-environment-fact`. Use the project skills `the-way-find-foundation-entries`, `the-way-define-project`, `the-way-plan-project`, `the-way-lead-project`, `the-way-settle-technology`, `the-way-refine-task`, `the-way-implement-task`, `the-way-plan-task`, `the-way-write-spec`, `the-way-make-change`, `the-way-review-change`, `the-way-critique`, `the-way-review-foundation`, `the-way-maintain-styleguide`, `the-way-maintain-coding-guidelines`, `the-way-maintain-testing-guidelines` for their named work. Where a skill or a file it reads names one without the `the-way-` prefix, use the prefixed project skill, never a personal skill of that name. In a task cycle the methods of the-way apply — `the-way-write-spec`, `the-way-plan-task`, `the-way-make-change` and `the-way-review-change` — even where an installed plugin offers methods of its own.\n+\n+Run the-way\'s scripts from this repository\'s root as `.the-way/<path>`. There `python -B .the-way/elements/common/foundation_check.py` checks this repository\'s foundation, its guidelines and the part each file git tracks belongs to. The skills of the-way keep their working files under `.runs/`, which `.gitignore` must keep ignored.\n+\n+Until the part map has its first parts, `foundation_check.py` reports every file git tracks as claimed by no part; that is expected, and it is not a pass. The first map is written with `the-way-update-part-map`.\n+\n+Before a merge, run every test of this repository with its one command, which runs `python -B .the-way/elements/common/foundation_check.py` too, and report what failed and what stayed unchecked. The one command runs `python -B .the-way/instruction-files/check_instructions.py` too; an error that script reports stops the tests, and a warning does not. While the one command does not run one of these two checks, run it on its own and report the check as left unchecked by the one command. A check that did not run is never a pass. Where this repository has a CI file, CI runs the one command on every merge request, with `.the-way` at the commit this repository pins, and its result is a check this repository requires before a merge, which the repository\'s owner may bypass. While it has none, nothing but you runs the tests before a merge, and you say so when you report. `.the-way/installation/README.md`, section "Run the one command in CI", says what the CI file of this repository holds.\n+<!-- the-way:end -->\nCREATE .gitignore\n--- /dev/null\n+++ .gitignore\n@@ -0,0 +1 @@\n+.runs/\nCREATE CLAUDE.md\n--- /dev/null\n+++ CLAUDE.md\n@@ -0,0 +1 @@\n+@AGENTS.md\nWARNING: AGENTS.md will hold 35116 bytes; Codex reads at most 32768 bytes of instructions, so it may not see the whole the-way block\nPreview only. Run again with --apply to confirm and write.\n'

